			<div class="content" id="post-content-4679981">
									

<p>First of all, I see multiple links back to what I assume is a self-insert. Second of all, how does a nebula exactly interact with items (writing/drawing)? Is it that she is condensed enough that it is solid? Are the items radioactive after they're taken away? How does the journal not degrade from the radiation? How do the researchers have access to said journal without getting radiation everywhere? Do they decontaminate it? If so, how? How do they do it without SCP-5827 becoming angered? Why does the anomaly even have access to a journal? Who knew that it was called &quot;Nebula&quot;? A POI? Why was this never described? If the 4th-century thing is supposed to explain this, why do they know that this is the specific anomaly?</p>
<p>Its allowed books, so is the journal considered a book? Is that why it's approved? What about writing utensils? Why is no more then 10kg of mass allowed?</p>
<p>There's also a grammar error, just for your knowledge. &quot;Stars, they are always very pretty.&quot; is not how that should be written. It should be written, in my opinion, as &quot;The stars I made are pretty, which is why I made a lot of them&quot;. Or something along those lines.</p>
<p>I may have missed the point of the article, and I admit so, but these just broke the immersion for me.</p>

