				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/rjb-r" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1229263); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1229263&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608238128" alt="RJB_R" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1229263)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/rjb-r" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1229263); return false;" >RJB_R</a></span> <span class="odate time_1591553017 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">07 Jun 2020 18:03</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4680419">
									

<p>I'm really struggling to find anything positive to say about this. I think it's absolutely dreadful. The central character, Nebula, does not read authentically as a child and even in the attempt I think it's a waste of potential to make this character who is directly and literally made of and making starstuff into such a non-entity. Lines like &quot;I can’t focus on anything but my body breaking. The stars want me back. I’m not doing my job.&quot; could work in a vacuum but in the context here it's very jarring to go back and forth from stuff like that to the little girl.</p>
<p>Other than the voice she's written in, the other major problem with Nebula is that she has no art, doesn't learn anything, and doesn't change because of the events of a story. She's a static character. Which is fine, you can write a static character in a way that works, but it's not working here. I don't think it was intentional, but the fact that the picture at the start and the picture at the end are palette swaps which don't actually show a significant change is more reflective of the actual article than was probably intended.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Nebula seems to be serving more as a vehicle to talk about the Dr. Kara character than herself. The journal entries are weighed down by Nebula repeatedly talking about how nice she is and her feelings towards this other character. It doesn't help that none of their interactions are particularly memorable or interesting. The SCP seems to be more concerned with promoting the Dr. Kara character than developing Nebula into her own person.</p>
<p>There are hints of character in the parts of the diary where she's not talking about Dr. Kara but that's only the March 17th section, the rest have to share space with talking up this other character who isn't even particularly relevant to the story. You could replace Dr. Kara with a generic Foundation scientist and the article would instantly become better because it would have more room for Nebula to show that she is her own person rather than a vehicle for showing how great another person is.</p>
<p>The Dr. Kara character contributes nothing and is actually detrimental to the article. All of her personal interactions with Nebula that aren't asking interview questions boil down to &quot;Oh you like that thing? I like it too!&quot; and the fact that Nebula seems to take so much meaning from these interactions cheapens the character because the audience doesn't care for these interactions, they don't add anything to drive a story or introduce conflict or anything else that makes character-driven stories fun to read.</p>
<p>I really dislike the picture, especially how it's very obvious that the second image is a palette swap of the first one. That's just my personal taste though, I don't think there's anything subjectively wrong with the image but the justification for why a photo can't be taken seems like more of an excuse to include the drawing than anything else, especially because it's not a core part of the article like, say, for Cassie where the SCP is literally a living drawing so there's not much else you could use as a picture.</p>
<p>There's a coming-of-age story in here somewhere but it's buried underneath a lack of relatability to the central character and too much space being taken up by a secondary character who really has no reason to be in the article at all. If you were to rewrite this I would remove Dr. Kara from it entirely, she adds nothing and every portion she is in detracts from the article, and try to make Nebula sound more like an actual little girl and not an approximation of one. Then focus more on her own internal struggle with this new thing happening to her. I think you could even keep the second image in the article as it makes sense in the second context of being a self-portrait in her journal. There are pieces here that could work. But it's just not working on any level right now.</p>
</div>
